    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Frontotemporal Dementia (FTD) is an aging-related neurodegenerative disease characterized by the preferential atrophy of the frontal and temporal lobes of the brain and the accumulation of protein aggregates. Recently, variants of the lysosomal transmembrane gene TMEM106B have been identified by a genome-wide association study as protective for FTD in humans. Though TMEM106B holds promise for future therapeutic purposes, it remains uncharacterized in the context of neurodegeneration with age, mostly due to the lack of appropriate and feasible vertebrate animal models for aging. A new model organism, the African turquoise killifish, is especially advantageous for addressing this question in aging due to the killifish’s short life-span, ease of genetic manipulation, and large number of offspring. Using CRISPR technology, we have created a knockout of tmem106b, the killifish ortholog of TMEM106B. Though we are unable to definitively conclude from our experiments whether the removal of tmem106b increases or decreases lysosomes in the killifish brain, preliminary data suggests that knocking out tmem106b in the killifish alters lysosomal levels of the brain differently depending on age. Preliminary data also suggests that the rate of lysosomal decline with age is slower in tmem106b knockout killifish than in wildtype killifish. In exploring the mechanisms of how TMEM106B protects against diseases of the aging brain, we may be able to target relevant pathways in the future to fight old-age dementia.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
